My current goal is to synthesize the library manager from the design and execute it. The synthesized program is what will be loaded into the implementation of the design. 

However as I am workign towards that, I am learning a lot of things which I want to address in my next interation of this laguage. So I am adding this folder as a place for me to leave notes as I identify them. 

Notes: 
- Is it right to call it a scripting language? It seems more approriate to call it a decleartive language.
- The invariants should be calculated from the pimitives, this way, the entire flow of the behavioral is configurable insteafd of hardcoding invariants.
- The last point is more general, it applies to all of the functionality, even when selecting the next behavior based on the world state, that can be done through the primitives. This process doesn't have to be convoluted, I just have to establish the right primitives to make all of this possible.
- In the long term, there will be a lexer that will generate the syntax tree that is created from the behavioral langugae and it will be compiled to identify errors. Here the compiler is checking the meaning to ensure it is valid before it is synthesized into a program.

# Planning

In this version, I will be more faithful to my initial plan of using Axiomatic Primitives to build Derived Primitives and then use those to build composite behaviors. In my first iteration, I will actually write the derived primitives using the axiomatic primitives (i.e. I won't be abstracting them yet). However, since I will be building them from the axiomatic primitives, establishing the abstraction later won't be difficult. I also am taking an incremental step and am not yet establishing a language with a lexer etc, I am still using basic regex parsing to work through the core functionality of synthesizing the program and executing it.

## Axiomatic Primitives

Ongoing list:
- Create Primitive
- Get Primitive
- Set Primitive
- Delete Primitive
- BinOp Primitive

## Functional Primitives:

Ongoing list:
- If block 
- This will contain the ability to execute logic based on the logical evaluation.
- Currently, this will only establish an if else block. In the end, multiple if statements are just nested if statements where the else blocks evaluates the next if condition, so making that leap later won't be difficult.


## Derived-Behavior-Lifecycle Primitives (upcoming)

These are functional blocks of a behavior lifecycle and they are derived from the atomic 
- Select

I will have a meta participant hold the result of the operation with a given convention. 

- select is `select_behavior `

I am essentially just writing the script with axiomatic primitives and then using meta participants to store the results. These aren't actual world participants, they just hold the metadata and the runtime can use it as needed. In this case, it determines the next behavior revealed by the world state.

TODO: Maybe calling it meta participant is actually confusing and I should think of another name.

## Derived-Behavior-Debugger Pirmitives

- Require 
- Invairant 

These are pirmitives that are invoked by the debugger to establish the correctness of the behavior. 

- require result is under `lifecycle_require_<name>` with the value being the result.
- invariant is `invariant_<participant>_<type>`.

As I am working through this new approach of using the synthesized program, I want to migrate the debugger to use the synthesized output to evaluate the correctness of the behavior. Ultimately, I want everything relevant to the behavior to be specified in the behavioral language, so this means invariants, require etc will have to be synthesized and evaluated. I feel that this approach is effective because it gives the developer complete control, it makes the engine itself just a computer and the develop establishes the intelligence.

